A||||10||horseman||no start with no codes...Help please!||||1||
Z||000000||horseman||06-25-2001||05:39 PM||||Car died. won't restart. turns over fine. I hear the fuel pumprelay kicking in. However,no(tiny little trickle or drops)fuel from the TBI. pulled fuse for pump to release fuel pressure from line. changed fuel filter. Still no start. still no codes! <BR>I don't have access to a pressure tester. any troubleshooting advice? Thanks in advance for the ideas. (84 2.5l,TBI,stick)||||reg||1
Z||000001||84Bill||06-25-2001||05:59 PM||||Got spark?<BR>If you do not and there is no gas. Replace the P/U coil in the distributer<p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 06-25-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000002||Tweek||06-25-2001||06:17 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>Car died. won't restart. turns over fine. I hear the fuel pumprelay kicking in.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Do you mean you can hear a single click, or a buzzing sould thats lasts about 2-3 sec.? Cause the buzzing is the fuel pump pressurizing the system<P>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>However,no(tiny little trickle or drops)fuel from the TBI. pulled fuse for pump to release fuel pressure from line. changed fuel filter.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>If you take the air cleaner cover off, and have someone try to start the car you should see the fuel spraying down, but it only does this while you are turning the motor over.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Rodrv6||06-25-2001||10:35 PM||||If you have spark and fuel pressure from the pump, but no fuel spray at the injector you may have a bad injector, wiring, or ecm.  The ecm on my 84 went south one day with the same symptoms--car died and wouldn't restart.  Everything else worked, but no fuel spray.  I jumpered the injector to the battery and got spray so I knew it was good.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||1
Z||000004||SteveJ||06-26-2001||07:53 AM||||When you remove the air cleaner as Tweek says to check for fuel flow bear in mind that the possibility exists that while you crank it a backfire may ignite the fuel and send a column of fire upward. Keep your face back where you can see but not directly over it. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||horseman||06-26-2001||10:03 AM||||Great ideas, many thanks. The relay is definetly working for 2-3sec. No fuel spray.<BR>Can the relay work and the fuel pump not? also, how can I jumper the injector to test it? Thanks again in advance.||||reg||5
Z||000006||horseman||06-26-2001||01:07 PM||||any other ideas guys?||||reg||4
Z||000007||Cliff Pennock||06-26-2001||01:11 PM||||If you hear the relay but not the fuel pump itself, then it's the fuel pump.<p>[This message has been edited by Cliff Pennock (edited 06-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||horseman||06-26-2001||03:28 PM||||Thanks Cliff,<BR>What is the difference in sounds?ie,how do I know which part that I am hearing? I detected a humm upon engaging the ignition to the on position. Would the relay just click or something if the pump is bad?||||reg||5
Z||000009||Rodrv6||06-26-2001||04:00 PM||||The hum is probably the pump running.  Do you get any spark while you're cranking the engine?  Take a spark plug and plug it in to one of the plug wires and ground the threaded part of the plug to the engine.  Have someone crank the engine and watch for a nice blue spark across the plug.  If the spark is ok, then take the air cleaner off, disconnect the plug on top of the fuel injector, attach a couple of wires to the terminals on the injector.  Turn the ignition "on" to pressurize the system and briefly connect the jumper wires to the battery terminals.  The injector is designed to operate on 12 volts, so you won't hurt it.  If you don't see a fuel spray, try reversing the wires at the battery.  If it sprays, the injector is good and the problem is probably in the wiring between the injector and the ecm or between the disributer and the ecm (no reference pulses from the distributer=no injector operation).  If the wires check out, then most likely the ecm itself has gone bad.  It can do this without setting any codes---Mine did that.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||1
Z||000010||Cliff Pennock||06-26-2001||06:32 PM||||Yes, the hum would be the fuel pump.||||reg||1
